From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 12:38:51 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: use x86 layouts for x86-64 ptrace register access

The x86-64 slice inherited arm64 ptrace layouts that do not match x86
tracers.  PTRACE_GETREGS wrote the 272-byte arm64 frame into gdb's
216-byte user_regs_struct and smashed its stack, PTRACE_GETFPREGS was
unimplemented, and NT_PRFPREG returned the 528-byte arm64 FPSIMD image.

Serve PTRACE_GETREGS, SETREGS, GETFPREGS and SETFPREGS from the x86
regsets, report FP state as a 512-byte FXSAVE image, and add the
NT_X86_XSTATE regset, which reports XCR0 in the software-reserved words
as x86 does.  i386 tasks expose, as Linux's i386 view does, the i387
FSAVE image as NT_PRFPREG, the FXSAVE image as NT_PRXFPREG, and
NT_X86_XSTATE.  The signal-frame code and the regsets share one
conversion between the task FP state and a standard XSAVE image, and
one between that image and the FSAVE image.

An i386 tracer's requests use the i386 layouts, as in Linux's
ia32_arch_ptrace(): PEEKUSR and POKEUSR over struct user32, GETREGS and
SETREGS, GETFPREGS and SETFPREGS as the FSAVE image, and GETFPXREGS and
SETFPXREGS as the FXSAVE image.  PTRACE_GET_THREAD_AREA and
SET_THREAD_AREA reach the task's i386 TLS entries for a tracer of
either ABI.

PTRACE_PEEKUSER and POKEUSER follow x86 struct user rules: other fields
read as zero, and the debug registers hold what x86 Linux keeps without
an armed breakpoint.  DR6 reads with its reserved bits set, DR7 reads as
last written, and DR0 to DR3 stage addresses under x86's length,
alignment and user-space checks.  UML cannot arm a hardware breakpoint,
so a DR7 value that enables one fails with EINVAL, as an invalid value
does on x86, and a debugger never believes one armed.  Exec clears the
debug registers, and a child inherits DR6 and DR7 but no staged
breakpoint.
---
 arch/arm64/um/asm/processor.h   |  34 +++
 arch/arm64/um/asm/ptrace-abi.h  |   3 +
 arch/arm64/um/asm/ptrace.h      |   8 +
 arch/arm64/um/ptrace.c          | 395 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 arch/arm64/um/signal.c          | 239 ++++++++-----------
 arch/arm64/um/x86_32_syscalls.c |  32 +++
 arch/arm64/um/x86_fpstate.h     | 107 +++++++++
 arch/um/kernel/ptrace.c         |  15 +-
 8 files changed, 672 insertions(+), 161 deletions(-)
 create mode 100644 arch/arm64/um/x86_fpstate.h

diff --git a/arch/arm64/um/asm/processor.h b/arch/arm64/um/asm/processor.h
index d070ff929a9f79d3d086df7ec6ed26180a193c69..6f1a05af8a7ff752fc39bd72fe6b168e40586d8c 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/um/asm/processor.h
+++ b/arch/arm64/um/asm/processor.h
@@ -7,6 +7,24 @@
 #include <linux/time-internal.h>
 #include <sysdep/faultinfo.h>
 
+#ifdef CONFIG_UML_X86_64_USERSPACE_ONLY
+/*
+ * The x86 debug registers a tracer sees.  UML arms no hardware breakpoint,
+ * so they hold only what x86 Linux keeps without one: DR0 to DR3 addresses
+ * staged for a disabled breakpoint, with the length and type its checks
+ * apply to them, DR6 as the last #DB left it, and DR7 as last written.
+ */
+struct uml_x86_64_debugregs {
+	unsigned long address[4];
+	unsigned long dr6;
+	unsigned long dr7;
+	/* Slots holding a staged breakpoint, and each one's DR7 length
+	 * and type field. */
+	unsigned char staged;
+	unsigned char control[4];
+};
+#endif
+
 struct arch_thread {
 	unsigned long tpidr_el0;
 	void *darwin_task;
@@ -15,6 +33,9 @@ struct arch_thread {
 	/* PR_SME_VL_INHERIT, which survives exec. */
 	bool sme_vl_inherit;
 #endif
+#ifdef CONFIG_UML_X86_64_USERSPACE_ONLY
+	struct uml_x86_64_debugregs debugregs;
+#endif
 };
 
 #define INIT_ARCH_THREAD { .tpidr_el0 = 0, .darwin_task = NULL, \
@@ -23,6 +44,11 @@ struct arch_thread {
 static inline void arch_flush_thread(struct arch_thread *thread)
 {
 	thread->tpidr_el0 = 0;
+#ifdef CONFIG_UML_X86_64_USERSPACE_ONLY
+	/* Like flush_ptrace_hw_breakpoint(): exec drops every breakpoint
+	 * and clears DR6 and DR7. */
+	thread->debugregs = (struct uml_x86_64_debugregs) { 0 };
+#endif
 }
 
 static inline void arch_copy_thread(struct arch_thread *from,
@@ -32,6 +58,14 @@ static inline void arch_copy_thread(struct arch_thread *from,
 #ifdef CONFIG_UML_ARM64_SME
 	to->sme_vl_inherit = from->sme_vl_inherit;
 #endif
+#ifdef CONFIG_UML_X86_64_USERSPACE_ONLY
+	/* Like x86 copy_thread(): a child inherits DR6 and DR7 but none of
+	 * the parent's breakpoints. */
+	to->debugregs = (struct uml_x86_64_debugregs) {
+		.dr6 = from->debugregs.dr6,
+		.dr7 = from->debugregs.dr7,
+	};
+#endif
 }
 
 #ifdef CONFIG_UML_ARM64_SME
diff --git a/arch/arm64/um/asm/ptrace-abi.h b/arch/arm64/um/asm/ptrace-abi.h
index 3afa6ed84585b358f068303471d9b3d640f20421..7af74c93f494f76334b7b6610fbe1ad56c8a58ee 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/um/asm/ptrace-abi.h
+++ b/arch/arm64/um/asm/ptrace-abi.h
@@ -2,8 +2,11 @@
 #ifndef _ASM_UM_ARM64_PTRACE_ABI_H
 #define _ASM_UM_ARM64_PTRACE_ABI_H
 
+/* The x86-64 slice serves these in subarch_ptrace() with x86 layouts. */
+#ifndef CONFIG_UML_X86_64_USERSPACE_ONLY
 #define PTRACE_GETREGS 12
 #define PTRACE_SETREGS 13
+#endif
 #define PTRACE_GETFPREGS 14
 #define PTRACE_SETFPREGS 15
 #define PTRACE_OLDSETOPTIONS 21
diff --git a/arch/arm64/um/asm/ptrace.h b/arch/arm64/um/asm/ptrace.h
index 50bb312cff0f0d9e22d268238c92cc5e6aa1a5be..f0ec293ba7b1a9bb5e977f0f6d0bdb83a2aee383 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/um/asm/ptrace.h
+++ b/arch/arm64/um/asm/ptrace.h
@@ -39,6 +39,13 @@ static inline long regs_return_value(struct pt_regs *regs)
 	return PT_REGS_SYSCALL_RET(regs);
 }
 
+#ifdef CONFIG_UML_X86_64_USERSPACE_ONLY
+/* The task's i386 TLS descriptors, as on Linux x86. */
+int ptrace_get_thread_area(struct task_struct *child, int index,
+			   void __user *address);
+int ptrace_set_thread_area(struct task_struct *child, int index,
+			   void __user *address);
+#else
 static inline int ptrace_get_thread_area(struct task_struct *child, int index,
 					 void __user *address)
 {
@@ -50,6 +57,7 @@ static inline int ptrace_set_thread_area(struct task_struct *child, int index,
 {
 	return -EIO;
 }
+#endif
 
